--TEST--
`=` is an expression whose value is what was written, so it chains and nests; every compound form is `$x = $x op e` over the same ten operators; and the target of a compound form is evaluated exactly once, which a subscript whose index is a call makes observable — counted across all four target kinds, `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/assignment
// Four things are pinned here. `=` is an expression, so `$a = $b = 3` writes
// both and `($a = 5) + 1` is 6. Each compound form is its long spelling
// written once. Every one of the four targets — a local, a property, a
// static property and an array element — takes the same forms. And the
// target of a compound form is worked out once: `Probe::at` counts each time
// an index is computed, so a second evaluation shows up in the count.

class Probe {
    public static uint $computed = 0;
    public static uint $shared = 0;
    public uint $held = 0;

    public static function at(uint $index): uint {
        Probe::$computed += 1;
        return $index;
    }
}

// `=` answers the value it wrote, so it chains right to left and nests.
int $a = 0;
int $b = 0;
$a = $b = 3;
int $c = ($a = 5) + 1;
echo $a, " ", $b, " ", $c, "\n";

// Every compound form over an `int`, applied in turn. None of them changes
// the target's type.
int $n = 10;
$n += 5;
$n -= 3;
$n *= 2;
$n %= 7;
$n **= 3;
$n <<= 1;
$n >>= 2;
$n &= 7;
$n |= 8;
$n ^= 1;
echo $n, "\n";

// `/=` is the one that needs a target wide enough for what `/` answers.
float $f = 9.0;
$f /= 2;
echo $f, "\n";

// The same operator reaches all four target kinds, and the four agree.
uint $local = 4;
Probe $object = new Probe();
$object->held = 4;
Probe::$shared = 4;
array<uint> $slots = [4, 0];
$local += 6;
$object->held += 6;
Probe::$shared += 6;
$slots[0] += 6;
uint $agreed = 0;
if ($local == 10) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if ($object->held == 10) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if (Probe::$shared == 10) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if ($slots[0] == 10) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
echo $agreed, " of 4\n";

// A compound form evaluates its target once. A plain read and a plain write
// each compute the index once, so the three statements below leave the
// counter at 3 rather than at 4.
Probe::$computed = 0;
$slots[Probe::at(1)] = 5;
$slots[Probe::at(1)] += 7;
echo $slots[Probe::at(1)], " ", Probe::$computed, "\n";

// A string target takes `.=` the same way, in a local and in an element.
string $line = "a";
$line .= "b";
array<string> $named = ["k" => "p"];
$named["k"] .= "q";
echo $line, " ", $named["k"], "\n";
--EXPECT--
5 3 6
12
4.5
4 of 4
12 3
ab pq
